Phaser 4.2.1, Arcade Physics with gravity y 600. The player can stand on a crate and push it
around:

```js
this.player = this.physics.add.sprite(100, 300, 'player');
this.crate = this.physics.add.sprite(100, 400, 'crate');
this.physics.add.collider(this.player, this.crate);
this.physics.add.collider(this.crate, this.ground);   // ground is a static group
this.physics.add.collider(this.player, this.ground);
```

Jump logic in `update()`:

```js
if (this.cursors.up.isDown && this.player.body.onFloor()) {
  this.player.setVelocityY(-400);
}
```

Jumping works from the ground but never from the top of the crate, though the player visibly
rests on it. Why, and how do I fix it while keeping the crate pushable sideways?

graders.evidence:
The skill confirmed what I suspected: this is how Arcade Physics behaves, not a bug in your code.

## Why

In Arcade, `body.onFloor()` just returns `body.blocked.down`. **`blocked` only gets set when a body hits something that can't move**: a static body, an immovable or non-pushable body, or the world bounds. Your ground is a static group, so jumping from it works.

The crate is an ordinary dynamic, pushable body. When two dynamic bodies collide, Arcade separates them and sets **`touching.down`** on the player, not `blocked.down`. So while the player rests on the crate, `onFloor()` stays `false` and the jump check never passes.

## Fix

Keep the crate dynamic and pushable, and widen the ground check to include `touching.down`:

```js
const body = this.player.body;
const grounded = body.blocked.down || body.touching.down;

if (this.cursors.up.isDown && grounded) {
  this.player.setVelocityY(-400);
}
```

`touching` is reset each physics step and refilled by the colliders. When you read it in `update()`, it reflects the most recent step, which is what you want here.

### What not to do

The usual advice is `this.crate.setImmovable(true)` or `setPushable(false)`. That makes `onFloor()` work on the crate, but it also stops the player from pushing the crate sideways, which you don't want.

### Optional: only count standing on top

`touching.down` is set only when the player's bottom edge meets something, so bumping the crate from the side won't count as grounded. If you want the check to be explicit, or to add a small amount of "coyote time", you can record it in the collider callback:

```js
this.physics.add.collider(this.player, this.crate, (player, crate) => {
  if (player.body.touching.down && crate.body.touching.up) {
    this.lastGroundedAt = this.time.now;
  }
});
```

One related thing you may notice: when the player lands on the crate, the crate takes part of the impact. Its collider with the ground stops it from sinking, so this is fine. If the crate jitters under the player, give it some mass or drag (`this.crate.setDragX(...)`, `this.crate.body.mass = 2`). Remember that drag only works while the crate's x-acceleration is zero.

